WEBVTT

00:00:00.192 --> 00:00:01.632
Welcome to This Explainer. Today,

00:00:02.016 --> 00:00:04.732
we're going to learn exactly
how to take the sheer,

00:00:04.732 --> 00:00:08.572
overwhelming chaos of raw information
and organize it into elegant,

00:00:08.572 --> 00:00:10.132
lightning-fast structures.

00:00:10.132 --> 00:00:13.852
We are kicking things off with
Lesson 01, Understanding Trees.

00:00:13.852 --> 00:00:15.932
If you've ever sat there
and wondered how your

00:00:15.932 --> 00:00:18.012
computer finds exactly
what you're looking for

00:00:18.012 --> 00:00:21.372
in literally a fraction of a second,
well, you're in the right place.

00:00:22.176 --> 00:00:23.676
Here is our roadmap for today.

00:00:23.676 --> 00:00:27.276
We'll be looking at data, data
structures, and algorithms.

00:00:27.276 --> 00:00:30.196
Then we'll compare linear
and hierarchical structures,

00:00:30.196 --> 00:00:33.196
bring down exactly what a
tree is and why it's useful,

00:00:33.196 --> 00:00:35.516
and finally, we'll dive
into the terminology.

00:00:35.516 --> 00:00:40.512
Roots, parents, children, siblings,
leaves, levels, depth, and height.

00:00:40.836 --> 00:00:41.568
Let's get to it.

00:00:42.432 --> 00:00:45.312
data, data structures, and algorithms.

00:00:45.888 --> 00:00:50.628
First up, we've got data. And data
is really just any raw information.

00:00:50.628 --> 00:00:54.240
It could be a number, a name,
a date, you name it. But here's

00:00:54.624 --> 00:00:56.348
the thing, on its own,

00:00:56.348 --> 00:01:00.808
data isn't actually very helpful.
Imagine a massive list of just raw data,

00:01:00.808 --> 00:01:04.548
like say a thousand names. If
it's just disorganized chaos,

00:01:04.548 --> 00:01:08.688
it is incredibly slow for a computer to
search through and find one specific person.

00:01:09.408 --> 00:01:13.056
So this brings us to a brilliant
solution. We need a system.

00:01:13.388 --> 00:01:16.828
Think of a physical library. The book's
just sitting there on the shelves.

00:01:16.828 --> 00:01:17.760
That's your data. The

00:01:18.144 --> 00:01:20.588
catalog system used to
actually arrange those books

00:01:20.588 --> 00:01:23.468
so you can find them, that
is your data structure.

00:01:23.468 --> 00:01:26.508
And finally, the specific
clear steps a librarian follows

00:01:26.508 --> 00:01:31.200
to find a requested book, or maybe to put
away return books, that's the algorithm.

00:01:31.568 --> 00:01:35.708
By combining a data structure to arrange
things with an algorithm to process them,

00:01:35.712 --> 00:01:39.152
we totally transform that
initial chaos into perfect order.

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:41.740
linear and hierarchical structures.

00:01:42.528 --> 00:01:45.329
Now, you can organize data
linearly, which just means

00:01:45.329 --> 00:01:47.708
items are arranged one
right after the other.

00:01:47.708 --> 00:01:49.795
Think of the songs in your
favorite playlist, or

00:01:49.795 --> 00:01:51.708
people standing in a
queue at a coffee shop.

00:01:52.416 --> 00:01:56.316
Every single item has exactly one
thing before it and one thing after it.

00:01:56.316 --> 00:01:57.024
Simple, right?

00:01:57.696 --> 00:02:01.996
Then we have hierarchical structures. This
is where items are arranged in levels,

00:02:01.996 --> 00:02:06.356
meaning some items sit logically underneath
others. A great everyday example of this

00:02:06.356 --> 00:02:07.968
is the folder system on your computer.

00:02:08.736 --> 00:02:12.768
A standard corporate company chart
actually perfectly visualizes a hierarchy.

00:02:13.116 --> 00:02:16.256
You can see how the CEO sits
right there at the very top level.

00:02:16.256 --> 00:02:19.896
Below the CEO, you've got different
departments, like sales and dev.

00:02:19.896 --> 00:02:23.256
And then below dev, you might have specific
teams, you know, like web and data.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:27.320
Each team reports up to one
specific manager above it.

00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:31.420
In this hierarchical structure, it's the
absolute foundation of what we call trees

00:02:31.420 --> 00:02:32.500
in computer science.

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:36.540
Alright, section 3. What a
tree is and why it's useful.

00:02:37.344 --> 00:02:40.204
Now, to actually be considered
a tree in computer science,

00:02:40.204 --> 00:02:43.004
a data structure has to follow
some pretty strict rules.

00:02:43.004 --> 00:02:44.924
First off, it's a collection of nodes,

00:02:44.924 --> 00:02:48.124
and each node is simply a little
container holding a value.

00:02:48.124 --> 00:02:50.924
These nodes are connected by
lines, which we call edges.

00:02:50.924 --> 00:02:54.924
A true tree has exactly one top node
that acts as the starting point.

00:02:54.924 --> 00:02:58.124
And crucially, this is important.
It never loops back on itself.

00:02:58.124 --> 00:03:01.164
It is strictly a one-way flow
of structure. No charcats.

00:03:02.208 --> 00:03:04.828
So why do we bother setting
up all these strict rules?

00:03:04.828 --> 00:03:09.628
Well, trees are incredibly useful because
they show hierarchy with crystal clarity.

00:03:09.628 --> 00:03:11.868
But even more importantly for performance,

00:03:11.868 --> 00:03:16.748
this specific shape allows a computer to
move from the top down to any specific item

00:03:16.748 --> 00:03:19.188
without having to check
every single thing in turn.

00:03:19.188 --> 00:03:22.868
It literally bypasses entire
branches of data it doesn't need,

00:03:22.868 --> 00:03:26.628
which drastically speeds up its ability
to find exactly what you requested.

00:03:26.628 --> 00:03:27.588
It's a real game changer.

00:03:28.416 --> 00:03:29.966
Finally, section 4.

00:03:29.966 --> 00:03:35.676
Roots, Parents, Children, Siblings,
Leaves, Levels, Depth, and Height.

00:03:36.576 --> 00:03:40.236
When you visualize a tree data structure,
you're going to notice a playful,

00:03:40.236 --> 00:03:43.136
kind of counterintuitive quirk of
computer science right off the bat.

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:45.460
They actually grow upside down.

00:03:45.460 --> 00:03:48.060
Yeah, we start at the very top
and we build our way downwards.

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:53.580
To navigate this upside-down structure,
we rely on familiar family terms.

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:56.460
A parent is a node that
has other nodes below it.

00:03:56.460 --> 00:03:59.420
A child is a know directly
below its parent.

00:03:59.420 --> 00:04:02.700
And nodes that share the exact same
parent, well, they're called siblings.

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:07.180
So if nodes B and C are the children
of parent node A, that makes B and

00:04:07.180 --> 00:04:08.140
C siblings to each other.

00:04:08.832 --> 00:04:12.092
Connected nodes form these parent
child pairs all the way down the line.

00:04:12.864 --> 00:04:16.344
Alongside those family terms, we
also borrow a bit from nature.

00:04:16.344 --> 00:04:20.844
We call that single exclusive starting
point at the very top, the root node.

00:04:20.844 --> 00:04:22.272
In this case, that's node A.

00:04:22.752 --> 00:04:26.932
On the flip side, a leaf is a node with
absolutely zero children below it.

00:04:26.932 --> 00:04:27.932
It's a dead end.

00:04:27.932 --> 00:04:32.812
So nodes D, E and C are leaves, sitting
at the very edge of our tree's branches.

00:04:33.696 --> 00:04:35.646
Okay, so now that we know the anatomy, how

00:04:35.646 --> 00:04:37.596
do we actually measure one of these trees?

00:04:37.596 --> 00:04:40.796
Well, we organize it into levels,
counting down from the top.

00:04:40.796 --> 00:04:44.736
But be careful here, the root
is always level 0, not 1.

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:48.841
When we want to find a specific node's
depth, we just count the physical

00:04:48.841 --> 00:04:52.128
number of edges it takes to get from
the root down to that specific node.

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:56.386
And if we want measure the overall tree's
height, we look for the longest path

00:04:56.386 --> 00:05:00.180
from the route all the way down to a
leaf and count the edges on that path.

00:05:00.180 --> 00:05:02.680
For the tree we've been looking
at, the height is exactly 2.

00:05:03.456 --> 00:05:05.712
Understanding these structures reveals the

00:05:05.712 --> 00:05:08.076
invisible architecture
of our digital world.

00:05:08.076 --> 00:05:10.527
It's exactly what turns the sheer chaos of

00:05:10.527 --> 00:05:13.036
raw data into the
lightning fast technology

00:05:13.036 --> 00:05:14.976
that you and I rely on
every single day. So,

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:17.436
the next time your
computer retrieves a file

00:05:17.436 --> 00:05:20.142
seemingly out of nowhere
in a fraction of a second,

00:05:20.142 --> 00:05:22.848
ask yourself, what kind of
tree made that possible?

00:05:23.616 --> 00:05:26.676
Thanks so much for joining me for
Lesson 01, and I'll see you next time.
